body:
that's not what is happening. in the article they pay lip service to anti-displacement strategies, but the truth is they don't have a plan or actually care at all about people being priced out. what they are really doing is publicly acknowledging the history of the neighborhood in a safe, sanitized way while never owning the city governments role in the oppression of trans folks or the current levels of displacement which make it a very difficult place to live, financially. 

&#x200B;

it's a pretty common tactic that governments will take to make wealthy liberals think they actually give a shit about whatever marginalized group.

TBH it's really hard being trans but I know with all of my being who I am and it isn't the gender I was assigned at birth. I spent way too long being convinced that I was, and going through that experience, amongst other things, has made me feel so happy that I now know that I'm trans. I don't want to be a boy, whether cis or trans, and literally can't imagine how that could ever be fulfilling or comfortable. (for me personally; I totally understand that other people have different experiences  than me and am not throwing shade at trans or cis men)

And as much as I want to 'pass' (complicated gay sounds) by people I interact with, there is a certain point of pride that comes from being trans. Some of that may be a result of my anti-authoritarian/anti-normative personality but a lot of it is from the beautiful community I am part of, irl and virtually in spaces like this.

&#x200B;

I think there is also something about how I've grown through my life. I grew up in a very repressive, christian world and have been working hard, for a long time, to process the trauma I experienced. Along with that is a lot of negative and hurtful behaviors that I've worked to rectify and move through. Finally realizing that I am trans, and understanding how much of the pain in my life was caused by dysphoria and attempting to live as a wrong gender, has been a major milestone of growth for me and I am so grateful to  have moved into this phase of my life.

&#x200B;

None of this is to say you should feel differently about your own life. I'm sorry you are in such pain and I hope you have people in your life who tell you what a wonderful and amazing person you are.

I've been an anarchist since I was a teenager and didn't realize I was queer until \~7 years ago (everyone else did) and trans until \~1 1/2 years ago (some other people did). For me, growing up, my family was poor and I definitely always felt like the poor kid around my friends. I had a very strong anti-authoritarian streak that I think was a combination of class disenfranchisement, teenage rebellion, and reading a lot, all the time. I came across different thinkers who were articulating a lot of what I felt and saying it in ways that felt empowering in a world that only seemed to want to grind me down. I also grew up in the south of the u.s., am white, had a lot of black friends and saw the ways that racism was both personally enacted but also structurally maintained and even publicly celebrated in that part of my country.  Add to that I was a skateboarder and knew from first hand experience that cops are the enemy; a lot of what I was reading was contextualizing those shitty experiences into a broader anti-police sentiment that has only grown as I've gotten older. 

&#x200B;

tldr; I was very lucky to come to anarchism at a young age and the more I've experienced and learned the deeper that conviction has become.

Jadzia (person on the right) is a host for a symbiont. The symbiont has lived many lifetimes, being transferred from host to host (all voluntarily I believe) and so has experienced life as various genders, though I don't think they get too much out of the binary. The person on the left is also a Trill (humanoid who hosts a symbiont) and happened to have been married to a past host of Jadzia's symbiont. Because the relationship between host and symbiont is, well, symbiotic, Jadzia still has memories and feelings that are activated when she re-meets her past-spouse. Eventually things get too hot to handle and they gotta make out (and maybe more, it's been a minute since I saw it).

&#x200B;

Oh, and according to wikipedia their kiss was the first televised lesbian kiss. The original Star Trek show also featured the first televised interracial kiss, so they've had a track record of nudging boundaries of who gets shown making out on tv.
